WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:05.017
Picture a program with a mail room.

2
00:00:05.017 --> 00:00:08.857
Other parts of the program drop messages into an out tray.

3
00:00:09.067 --> 00:00:13.191
A helper thread, the relay, empties the tray and sends what it finds.

4
00:00:13.433 --> 00:00:16.448
When the tray is empty, it naps, then looks again.

5
00:00:16.667 --> 00:00:18.249
One day you try to quit.

6
00:00:18.249 --> 00:00:21.544
The main thread asks the relay to stop, and waits.

7
00:00:21.767 --> 00:00:25.585
The relay prints one line, relay interrupted, carrying on.

8
00:00:25.585 --> 00:00:28.351
And it carries on, until you press Ctrl+C.

9
00:00:28.567 --> 00:00:30.890
Polite, honest, and no help at all.

10
00:00:30.890 --> 00:00:33.679
It heard the request, and then it lost it.

11
00:00:34.500 --> 00:00:36.980
So, two questions you might be shy to ask.

12
00:00:36.980 --> 00:00:39.401
When a thread isn't running, where is it?

13
00:00:39.401 --> 00:00:42.648
And when you ask it to stop, where does the request go?

14
00:00:43.867 --> 00:00:45.917
Think of the relay as a clerk.

15
00:00:45.917 --> 00:00:48.445
A clerk's day has a few places to be.

16
00:00:48.667 --> 00:00:49.516
Not in yet.

17
00:00:49.516 --> 00:00:50.984
At a desk, working.

18
00:00:50.984 --> 00:00:52.992
Napping with an alarm set.

19
00:00:52.992 --> 00:00:54.923
Waiting for the doorbell.

20
00:00:54.923 --> 00:00:57.086
Stuck outside a locked door.

21
00:00:57.086 --> 00:00:57.859
Gone home.

22
00:00:58.067 --> 00:01:00.474
A thread has six places just like these.

23
00:01:00.474 --> 00:01:04.928
Java calls them states, and getState() tells you which one a thread is in.

24
00:01:05.133 --> 00:01:11.455
The clerk is the thread, and a desk is a core, the part of the processor that runs one thread at a time.

25
00:01:11.667 --> 00:01:16.351
new Thread gives us an object and nothing more: no real thread yet.

26
00:01:16.351 --> 00:01:17.120
That's NEW.

27
00:01:17.333 --> 00:01:21.816
start() asks the operating system for a real thread, which calls run().

28
00:01:21.816 --> 00:01:24.152
Now the relay is RUNNABLE, at a desk.

29
00:01:24.367 --> 00:01:28.917
RUNNABLE also covers a thread that's ready, but waiting for a free desk.

30
00:01:29.133 --> 00:01:33.809
The tray is empty, so the relay calls sleep, for 200 milliseconds.

31
00:01:33.809 --> 00:01:35.013
Where does it go?

32
00:01:37.633 --> 00:01:40.200
TIMED_WAITING: a wait with a time limit.

33
00:01:40.200 --> 00:01:42.831
When the time is up, it's RUNNABLE again.

34
00:01:43.033 --> 00:01:48.139
Now say the relay reads from a queue, whose take() waits until there's something to take.

35
00:01:48.139 --> 00:01:53.073
On an empty queue, there's no clock, only a doorbell: another thread adding a message.

36
00:01:53.300 --> 00:01:57.168
That's WAITING, and join() with no time limit waits the same way.

37
00:01:57.400 --> 00:02:01.204
So where was main, the whole time it sat in relay.join()?

38
00:02:03.833 --> 00:02:06.507
WAITING, right there, until the relay ends.

39
00:02:06.733 --> 00:02:08.873
The relay goes back to its desk.

40
00:02:09.100 --> 00:02:14.177
It also writes each message into a shared logbook, inside a synchronized block.

41
00:02:14.177 --> 00:02:17.327
That block is a door with one key, called a lock.

42
00:02:17.533 --> 00:02:21.639
If another thread holds the key, the relay waits outside the door.

43
00:02:21.639 --> 00:02:22.573
That's BLOCKED.

44
00:02:22.573 --> 00:02:25.745
When the key comes free, it takes it and goes back.

45
00:02:25.967 --> 00:02:28.919
And when run() returns, the thread is TERMINATED.

46
00:02:28.919 --> 00:02:32.593
That's final, and it's the one place our relay never gets to.

47
00:02:33.500 --> 00:02:35.693
Let's not take my word for it.

48
00:02:35.693 --> 00:02:40.079
The lesson's ThreadStates demo asks getState() at each stop.

49
00:02:40.079 --> 00:02:42.053
On JDK 21, it printed this.

50
00:02:42.267 --> 00:02:44.901
Each state shows up where the walk said.

51
00:02:44.901 --> 00:02:48.853
Notice the odd line out: waiter interrupted, flag now false.

52
00:02:48.853 --> 00:02:49.775
Hold on to it.

53
00:02:50.600 --> 00:02:53.717
And our relay spends nearly its whole life asleep.

54
00:02:53.717 --> 00:02:54.964
Honestly, relatable.

55
00:02:54.964 --> 00:02:58.705
But it means a stop request has to reach it while it sleeps.

56
00:02:59.533 --> 00:03:02.696
Java can't drag a thread away from its desk.

57
00:03:02.696 --> 00:03:03.846
It can only ask.

58
00:03:03.846 --> 00:03:06.145
relay.interrupt() is the asking.

59
00:03:06.367 --> 00:03:09.637
Picture a note on the clerk's desk: please finish up.

60
00:03:09.867 --> 00:03:14.741
If the clerk is napping, the note wakes them: sleep throws an InterruptedException.

61
00:03:14.741 --> 00:03:18.029
If they're busy, they see it the next time they look up.

62
00:03:19.233 --> 00:03:21.039
Here's the relay's catch block.

63
00:03:21.039 --> 00:03:24.068
The note wakes the relay, and it lands in the catch.

64
00:03:24.300 --> 00:03:26.120
Is the note still on the desk?

65
00:03:28.733 --> 00:03:28.943
No.

66
00:03:28.943 --> 00:03:33.769
Waking up tears the note off: sleep clears the flag before it throws.

67
00:03:33.769 --> 00:03:36.497
That's the demo's line, flag now false.

68
00:03:36.700 --> 00:03:41.144
So the relay prints, goes back to sleep, and nothing remembers the request.

69
00:03:41.367 --> 00:03:45.867
Once you've caught the exception, you hold the only copy of the request.

70
00:03:45.867 --> 00:03:53.241
So either let it travel up to your caller, or stick the note back on: Thread.currentThread().interrupt(), then return.

71
00:03:53.467 --> 00:03:56.581
Put back, the note stays where the relay can see it.

72
00:03:56.581 --> 00:03:58.856
Its proper name is the interrupt flag.

73
00:03:59.067 --> 00:04:03.608
Now the relay sees the note, finishes the batch in hand, and returns.

74
00:04:03.608 --> 00:04:06.504
TERMINATED at last, and main's join returns.

75
00:04:06.733 --> 00:04:08.522
The relay goes home on time.

76
00:04:08.522 --> 00:04:10.693
Somebody in that mail room should.

77
00:04:11.500 --> 00:04:13.965
Here is the fix in Java and in Kotlin.

78
00:04:13.965 --> 00:04:18.895
Kotlin doesn't force you to catch this exception, so add the try on purpose.

79
00:04:19.100 --> 00:04:21.639
The rule: never swallow an interrupt.

80
00:04:21.639 --> 00:04:23.423
Rethrow it, or restore it.

81
00:04:24.733 --> 00:04:25.699
Two more traps.

82
00:04:25.699 --> 00:04:30.336
A relay that's always busy never reaches sleep, so no exception arrives.

83
00:04:30.336 --> 00:04:34.908
It must look for the note itself, once per batch, with isInterrupted().

84
00:04:35.133 --> 00:04:39.215
And the static Thread.interrupted() reads the note, and tears it off.

85
00:04:41.233 --> 00:04:43.907
So where is a thread when it isn't running?

86
00:04:43.907 --> 00:04:47.451
In one of six places, and the call it made decides which.

87
00:04:47.451 --> 00:04:49.877
And a stop request is a note on a desk.

88
00:04:49.877 --> 00:04:51.991
It's easy to lose, so put it back.

89
00:04:52.200 --> 00:04:53.127
Quick check.

90
00:04:53.127 --> 00:04:56.989
A thread is inside wait, with a five second limit.

91
00:04:56.989 --> 00:04:57.916
Which place?

92
00:05:00.733 --> 00:05:01.690
TIMED_WAITING.

93
00:05:01.690 --> 00:05:06.336
Same call, plus a time limit, and the thread moves to the other box.
